append("host/ppe_host/clock.py", "class ScaledClock:\n    \"\"\"S7-3 (ADR 0059): the Clock of REPLAY. Time starts at the base clock's values when it is made and then runs `speed` times as fast as the base (1, 4 or 16), so every host timer that reads\n    it (15 s / 60 s link loss, 30 s stillness, 10 s CO hysteresis, 120 s heat, the dedup window) runs at replay speed. pause() stops it (neither wall_ms nor mono_ms moves), resume() starts it again.\n    wall_ms is the wall time at the start plus the scaled elapsed time; it does not follow a step of the base's wall clock (an NTP step cannot reorder a replay). The OS watchdog and its liveness stamps\n    must NOT use this clock: a 3 s stall limit read at 16x would be 0.19 s of real time (Runtime keeps the base clock for them).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, base, speed=1):\n        if isinstance(speed, bool) or not isinstance(speed, (int, float)) or not speed > 0: raise ValueError('speed must be a number above 0')\n        import threading\n        self.base, self.speed, self._lock = base, speed, threading.Lock()\n        self._mono0, self._wall0 = base.mono_ms(), base.wall_ms()\n        self._acc, self._since, self._paused = 0.0, self._mono0, False      # scaled ms elapsed up to the last pause or resume, and the base mono_ms of the last resume\n    def _elapsed(self): return self._acc if self._paused else self._acc + (self.base.mono_ms() - self._since) * self.speed\n    def mono_ms(self):\n        with self._lock: return self._mono0 + int(self._elapsed())\n    def wall_ms(self):\n        with self._lock: return self._wall0 + int(self._elapsed())\n    @property\n    def paused(self): return self._paused\n    def pause(self):\n        with self._lock:\n            if not self._paused: self._acc = self._elapsed(); self._paused = True\n    def resume(self):\n        with self._lock:\n            if self._paused: self._since = self.base.mono_ms(); self._paused = False\n    def now(self): return now_of(self)\n");
append("docs/PROGRESS.md", "- Part S7-3 done (ADR 0059; S7-4 would be the Pause control, which needs a new endpoint): REPLAY feeds a stored session through the normal pipeline. clock.ScaledClock (1x, 4x, 16x, pause and resume; the OS watchdog and liveness keep the real clock). services/replay.py (open_source and label_of; a mode=ro connection to db_path, so the original is never written; the frames and base_health rows of the session merged by time, a step back counts as no time and one step is cut to 300 s; ReplayThread delivers each item when the scaled clock reaches its offset, starting when the gate goes live, with pause(), resume(), finished and on_done; at the end the clock stops). runtime.py: Runtime(replay=(session_id, speed)), REPLAY mode only, with real_clock beside the scaled clock. main.py: --session N (required in REPLAY) and --speed 1|4|16, both refused in other modes (exit 2); check_replay (exit 2 for a missing database, missing session, session with no frame, or open alerts or unresolved episodes in the test database); begin_session never resumes a REPLAY session (SESSION_ABANDONED detail new_replay); the new session's session_label names the source. No migration, setting, wire, endpoint or constants change. test_replay.py: 45 tests. Observed with the real clock: link_loss at 16x raised signal_lost at 38 s and link_lost at 82 s of source time (35 and 80 at 1x), man_down at 4x at 54 s as at 1x; the 1 Hz tick makes silence rules up to speed seconds late (ADR 0059).\n");
